# The layer dimension as the checker reaches it. One row per filed layer: how many rules examine
# it, how many found a violation, and whether anything reaches it at all.
#
# `rank/incidence_reach.sqlc`, transposed
#   The transpose is the part that names things. That relation counts, per relation, how much of
#   the layer dimension it touches. It is an aggregate per relation, so it can say how many and
#   never which, and a layer nothing reaches leaves no row anywhere to be counted. This is the
#   same measurement read down the other axis, where the subject is the layer and the shortfall
#   has a name. It is the third member of the reach family, after `incidence_reach` and
#   `guard_reach`.
#
# It needs a key that resolves
#   It depends on the second arm of `checks/grain.sqlc`. A rule that puts a claim address in the
#   contract's `layer` column names no layer, and this relation joined on that key drops those
#   pairs and prints a believable table that reports layers as unreachable when they are not: an
#   artifact of the mis-declared subject rather than a fact about the model. A cover measured
#   through a key that does not resolve measures the key.
#
# Depth, not a boolean
#   The useful reading is depth. On this corpus every filed layer is reached by at least one
#   rule, which makes `reaches = 0` a guard that can fire rather than a finding. What varies is
#   how much: a layer one rule can speak about is a layer one repair silences, and that is
#   visible here and nowhere else.
#
# `examined` counts rules, not rows
#   On purpose. A claim-grained rule returns many rows for one layer, and counting those would
#   grade a layer by how many claims it happens to file rather than by how many different
#   questions this repository can put to it.
#
# A rule that examined nothing does not reach this layer
#   `checks/all.sqlc` joins its roster first so a rule with an empty population still returns a
#   row, with `violates` NULL and no filing. Those rows carry no layer, so they cannot arrive
#   here, which is the vacuous verdict staying where it belongs: a fact about the rule, not about
#   the layer.
-- checks/all.sqlc counted per layer, against layers/every_layer.sqlc as the whole dimension.
SELECT l.filing,
       l.layer,
       count(DISTINCT c.rule)                                   AS examined_by,
       count(DISTINCT c.rule) FILTER (WHERE c.violates)         AS violated,
       count(DISTINCT c.rule) = 0                               AS nothing_reaches_it
FROM      (
    :compose(layers/every_layer.sqlc)
) l
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(checks/all.sqlc)
) c ON c.filing = l.filing AND c.layer = l.layer
GROUP BY l.filing, l.layer
